223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 498 0 R
/E (Stage 1 - Localization \(easy\): many edges, direct binding signal. The model learns the broad shape of "which TFs can reach which genes.")
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (Stage 2 - Perturbation \(harder\): fine-tune on functional/causal edges, so the model learns to tell binding apart from actual regulation.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 500 0 R
/E (Stage 3 - Dual-evidence \(hardest\): fine-tune on the small, high-confidence set \(both binding and functional\). Here we freeze the encoders and only refine the graph/decoder, to avoid overwriting good features with signal from very few examples.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 501 0 R
/E (Why do this? The most valuable tier \(dual-evidence\) is far too small to train a model from scratch. Curriculum learning lets the earlier, larger tiers provide a strong warm start, so by the time we reach the hard tier the model only needs to specialize, not learn from nothing.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
